<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antofagasta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antofagasta is a port city in northern Chile and capital of the Antofagasta Region, on the edge of the Atacama Desert. Founded under Bolivian rule in 1868 to export nitrates, it was occupied by Chile in February 1879, which opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, and it passed to Chile with the whole Bolivian coast. Today it is Chile's main port for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antofagasta.jpg" alt="Antofagasta" width="320" height="554" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Montaje realizado por Açipni-Lovrij. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montaje_de_Antofagasta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Antofagasta lies about 1,100 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on a coastal plain, south of the Mejillones Peninsula and north of Cerro Coloso, at an average elevation of 40 metres. The urban area begins abruptly, breaking the monotony of the desert. The Tropic of Capricorn passes north of the city, near the Cerro Moreno International Airport, and a monument opened on 21 December 2000 marks it and works as a solar calendar. The name is presumed to derive from that of Antofagasta de la Sierra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catamarca-province">Catamarca Province</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 18th century the territory appeared on maps of the Captaincy General of Chile, depending on Copiapó, and it was disputed between Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> until the Boundary Treaty of 1866. On 18 September 1866 José Santos Ossa and Francisco Puelma obtained a Bolivian concession over nitrate lands after finding rich saltpeter deposits at the Salar del Carmen, and formed the Sociedad Exploradora del Desierto de Atacama. On 8 May 1872 the Bolivian government designated Antofagasta a Puerto Mayor, opening it to world trade.</p><p>In 1873 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> signed a secret defensive alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and a contract with a Chilean company, the Compañía de Salitres y Ferrocarril de Antofagasta, which Bolivia's congress never ratified. In 1874 Chile and Bolivia signed a border treaty that barred new taxes on Chilean capital for 25 years. In the Bolivian version of events, congress imposed a tax of 10 cents because the contract was not in force; when Bolivia reactivated the tax after a Chilean note, it cancelled the company's concession. Chile occupied Antofagasta on 14 February 1879, which triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> (1879–83). The Treaty of Peace and Friendship, signed on 20 October 1904 and promulgated on 21 March 1905, fixed the border permanently.</p><p>A British community erected a clock tower in 1912 that replicates Big Ben, in the Plaza Colón. A mudslide on 18 June 1991 killed 92 people, left 16 missing and about 20,000 homeless, and destroyed 493 buildings; an earthquake of magnitude 7.3 struck on 30 July 1995.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Antofagasta is the capital of Antofagasta Province and Antofagasta Region, with a population of 401,096 at the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a port and the chief service hub of one of Chile's major mining areas, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and saltpeter were historically important and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> has been by far the most important mineral since the mid-19th century. It is also a university city, home to the Catholic University of the North and the University of Antofagasta.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antofagasta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the War of the Pacific of 1879–1884, which began when Bolivia raised taxes on a Chilean nitrate company in <b>Antofagasta</b>, Chile occupied the entire coast within weeks, and Bolivian troops withdrew from the war after the Battle of Tacna in 1880.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Pacific reshaped the map of South America, leaving Bolivia without a coastline, a loss Bolivia has never accepted, and giving Chile the provinces of Tarapacá and <b>Antofagasta</b>, while the dispute over Tacna and Arica was settled with Peru only by the Treaty of Lima of 1929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
